WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.281
Your Apple Watch

00:00:01.093 --> 00:00:01.883
app works in

00:00:01.694 --> 00:00:02.625
the simulator

00:00:02.765 --> 00:00:03.884
Then crashes

00:00:04.422 --> 00:00:05.660
An indie developer's

00:00:05.483 --> 00:00:06.403
Apple Watch app

00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:06.980
ran fine in

00:00:06.792 --> 00:00:07.676
the simulator

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.584
but wouldn't even

00:00:08.396 --> 00:00:09.363
build for real

00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:09.940
watches

00:00:10.462 --> 00:00:11.263
The reason

00:00:11.464 --> 00:00:12.525
older Apple Watch

00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:14.306
models use arm64

00:00:14.129 --> 00:00:15.462
underscore 32

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:16.676
where Swift's Int

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.879
is only thirty-two

00:00:17.691 --> 00:00:18.339
bits

00:00:18.478 --> 00:00:19.621
A hash constant

00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:20.470
simply didn't fit

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:22.499
Worse twenty-seven

00:00:22.311 --> 00:00:23.949
spots turned milliseconds

00:00:23.761 --> 00:00:24.539
into an Int

00:00:24.351 --> 00:00:25.188
that would crash

00:00:24.999 --> 00:00:25.836
on launch

00:00:25.975 --> 00:00:26.848
The lesson

00:00:27.049 --> 00:00:28.145
always build for

00:00:27.956 --> 00:00:28.935
real hardware

00:00:29.454 --> 00:00:30.468
Full story on

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:31.175
the dashboard
